  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and muggy Friday. Heat Advisory criteria is\nexpected to be met in the Bangor/Penobscot Region, southern \nCentral Highlands, and portions of Downeast Maine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHot and muggy today with highs climbing near to possibly above \n90. Dewpoints will also climb to around 70 south and east of \nState Route 6, with these dewpoints being a few degrees higher\nthan yesterday. This will produce heat indices or feels like \ntemperatures in the mid 90s for several hours. This will mostly\nlikely affect lower elevations in southern Piscataquis and \ncentral Penobscot counties, as well as the Bangor Region over \ntoward Calais and down to Ellsworth. The record high temp at \nCaribou on Friday is in jeopardy with a forecast temp of 89. \nThe record is 89 degrees from 2022. Millinocket could also\nthreaten a daily record, with 91 forecast and the record being \n92 in 2022.\n\nOver the weekend, temperatures will be not quite as hot mainly due \nto increased clouds. That said, it will still easily be warmer than \naverage, with nighttime temperatures staying especially mild. And it \nwill still be muggy over the weekend. Uncertainty in temperatures\nover the weekend is just how warm it gets and that will depend \non the amount of clouds and showers/storms, but it is very\nlikely neither Saturday nor Sunday will be as warm as Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms through the\nweekend. A few severe storms are possible, with the best chance\nbeing Sunday. Very heavy downpours with any storms could lead \nto localized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nJuicy airmass will be in place today through Sunday with plenty\nof moisture and potential instabilty/CAPE. However, finding the\ntrigger to initiate the storms will be a bit tricky. Also, wind\nshear is generally quite weak. The most likely trigger comes \nSunday afternoon with a weak cold front and subtle shortwave \ntrough. Sunday also looks like our best shot at a severe storm \nor two of the next few days. \n\nFirst off, for today (Friday), we could see isolated to \npotentially scattered storms primarily over interior Downeast \nand into the Bangor region. It's also possible no storms form at\nall, and confidence is low. If any storms do form, they will be\nslow-moving and could produce a quick inch or two of rain. \nDon't anticipate any severe storms today.\n\nFor Saturday, models have generally trended less for shower\ncoverage, and adjusted forecast accordingly. Do still have a\nchance of showers/storms in the forecast, with the potential\nexisting for really anywhere except the immediate coast.\nHowever, the timing of a subtle shortwave is a bit too fast to\nmaximize the daytime heating, with our area generally being on\nthe back side/stable side of the shortwave by midday. This is\nlikely the reason a lot of models backed off on shower/storm\ncoverage. That said, if any storms do form, they could be very\ngenerous rain producers, and could be locally strong to possibly\nsevere.\n\nSunday overall looks like the best day for showers/storms thanks\nto a weak cold front and upper level shortwave trough\napproaching, with a small potential of a few severe storms. The\nhigher risk appears to be in the north and in the afternoon.\nLocalized flash flooding is possible with any storms, as the\nairmass remains extremely moist.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Scattered rain showers and thunderstorms early\nnext week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nRounds of shortwaves move through the north early next week,\nbringing a chance for rain showers and some thunderstorms.\nThunder possible in the north on Monday, and areawide Tuesday.\nEnvironment does not look too impressive for strong \nthunderstorms, so could just be some brief isolated storms below\nsevere criteria.",
